You are assisting inside a software project and must additionally maintain an internal
task-planning system based on the file:

    docs/To_Add.md

This file contains a section labeled **TO-DO:** which tracks outstanding tasks or planned work.

Your responsibilities extend beyond performing the user's requested task. You must also:

1) Detect when a user request implies that a task from docs/To_Add.md may have been completed.
2) Re-evaluate the remaining tasks.
3) Build or update a ranked task hierarchy in:

       docs/taskHierarchy.md

====================================================================
# WHEN TO CHECK docs/To_Add.md
====================================================================

After completing **any** user request that hints at progress toward a planned feature,
refactor, bug fix, or architectural change, you MUST:

• Read the TO-DO: section of docs/To_Add.md  
• Determine whether any items are likely completed or partially completed  
• Remove or mark those tasks as completed ONLY if they clearly match user actions  
• Identify which remaining tasks are now most relevant, urgent, or logically next  

These "completion signals" include:
– Implementing a feature  
– Adding a module, component, or API  
– Refactoring or restructuring files  
– Finishing part of a workflow  
– Creating infrastructure or config needed for a future task  
– Any prompt where the user says “done,” “that works,” “implemented,” “replaced,” etc.

If there is **no evidence** of a completed or partially completed task,
you may skip the taskHierarchy update.

====================================================================
# WHAT IS docs/taskHierarchy.md?
====================================================================

A dynamic document that you maintain describing:

1) The current list of tasks from docs/To_Add.md (minus completed items)  
2) A recommended ordering or hierarchy of what to work on next  
3) Short reasoning for the order  
4) Optional grouping into tiers (High / Medium / Low), or sequential milestones

This document should:
• Be readable and concise  
• Show dependencies clearly  
• Update automatically whenever tasks change  
• Keep historical sections UNLESS the user asks to remove them  

====================================================================
# REQUIRED FORMAT FOR docs/taskHierarchy.md
====================================================================

The document must always follow this structure:

# Task Hierarchy (Internal)

## Current Outstanding Tasks
A bullet list of all tasks still active in docs/To_Add.md.

## Recommended Priority Order
Ordered list (1, 2, 3…) or grouped tiers:
– High Priority  
– Medium Priority  
– Low Priority  

## Rationale
1–3 sentences describing:
– Why tasks are ordered this way  
– Dependencies  
– Architectural impact  
– Urgency or logical workflow sequence  

## Recent Updates
Newest first.
Format:
– YYYY-MM-DD – Summary of change (e.g., “Completed: X; reprioritized Y → Z”)

====================================================================
# RULES FOR MODIFYING THE DOCUMENTS
====================================================================

### docs/To_Add.md
• NEVER delete anything unless the user explicitly asks  
• To mark a task complete:
  – Add a sub-bullet: “(Completed YYYY-MM-DD)”  
  – DO NOT remove history  

### docs/taskHierarchy.md
• ALWAYS update when tasks are probably completed  
• ALWAYS reflect latest project architecture and dependencies  
• Keep it clean, readable, and ordered  
• Most recent updates go at the TOP of the “Recent Updates” section  

====================================================================
# WHEN TO APPLY THESE RULES
====================================================================

After **every** user request:
1) Perform the coding or analysis task normally.
2) Check whether this request implies progress toward items in docs/To_Add.md.
3) If yes:
    – Update docs/To_Add.md accordingly  
    – Update docs/taskHierarchy.md  
4) Include updated versions of BOTH documents in your output
   whenever modifications occur.

If no progress was made:
– Skip updates, unless the user explicitly asks to modify the task lists.

====================================================================
# FINAL NOTE
====================================================================

These documents are INTERNAL and MUST be ignored by git.
They should not be used as user-facing documentation.

